Short:        Conformance test suite for P96 RTG drivers
Author:       Bernie Innocenti <bernie@codewiz.org>
Uploader:     Bernie Innocenti <bernie@codewiz.org>
Type:         dev/misc
Version:      @VERSION@
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0
Requires:     68020 or better; Picasso96 or CyberGraphX
Distribution: Aminet
Kurz:         Konformitaetstests fuer P96-RTG-Treiber

p96cts checks what a graphics driver actually draws. Each testcase renders a
scene -- lines, rectangles, patterned and masked fills, blits, scaled bitmaps,
scrolls, pixel arrays -- and compares it pixel by pixel against a golden
reference image captured from P96's own software rasterizer. A driver that
gets the geometry, the draw modes, the write mask or the pattern phase wrong
produces different pixels, and the suite says which pixels and by how much.

Runs are non-interactive and the exit code reflects the result, so it works as
an automated check. A failing testcase writes two PNGs beside the goldens:
what it rendered, and the differing pixels in red over the golden dimmed to
gray. A red result is evidence, so please report it -- failures found this way
have been fixed in amiberry, Copperline, AROS and P96 itself.

It also runs on native AGA screens, where the bitmaps are planar and the
comparison puts graphics.library's own rendering against the same reference,
and on AROS, where it falls back to cybergraphics.library.

Project's home page with documentation, test results and issue tracker:

  https://github.com/codewiz/p96cts

Source of this release:

  https://github.com/codewiz/p96cts/tree/v@VERSION@


Usage
-----

  p96cts <monitor> <WxHxD>     one mode, e.g. p96cts PicassoII 640x480x8
  p96cts <monitor>             every depth the monitor offers
  p96cts softrast 320x200x8    P96's software rasterizer, the reference
  p96cts -h                    the full argument list

Activate an RTG board's monitor first (DEVS:Monitors or SYS:Storage/Monitors);
only uaegfx registers itself.

Run it from inside the P96CTS drawer: the references in golden/ and the picture
the BitMapScale scenes scale in logo/ are both found relative to the current
directory.

